<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Constance</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bodensee</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Constance is a large lake on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> at the northern foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Its shores were settled by the Alemanni and later filled with monasteries such as Reichenau and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-gall">Saint Gall</a>, the bishopric of Constance and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">free imperial cities</a> including Lindau and Überlingen. Trade across and around the lake linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-constance.jpg" alt="Lake Constance" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Earth Science and Remote Sensing Unit, Lyndon B. Johnson Space Center · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_Constance_Edited.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lake Constance consists of three bodies of water on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> at the northern foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>: the Upper Lake, or Obersee, the Lower Lake, or Untersee, and the connecting stretch of the Rhine called the Seerhein. The Alpine Rhine enters from the south and the High Rhine leaves westward. The lake is 63 kilometres long, nearly 14 kilometres wide at most, covers about 536 square kilometres and lies 395 metres above sea level; its maximum depth, 252 metres, is in the middle of the Upper Lake. Its shoreline of 273 kilometres runs through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, and the exact location of the borders within the lake has never been settled between the three states.</p><p>The largest island is Reichenau in the Lower Lake. Lindau and Mainau lie in the Upper Lake, and the large peninsula of Bodanrück separates the two lakes. The largest towns on the Upper Lake are Constance, Friedrichshafen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bregenz">Bregenz</a>, Lindau, Überlingen and Kreuzlingen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The lake was formed by the Rhine Glacier during the Quaternary glaciation. About 10,000 years ago, after the last glacial period, the Obersee and Untersee still formed one lake. Hunters and gatherers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> frequented the area without settling, and the first lasting settlements, pile dwellings and wetland villages, date from the middle and late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>. A pile-dwelling village at Unteruhldingen has been reconstructed as an open-air museum. In 2015 a 20-kilometre line of about 170 man-made underwater stone mounds, dated to the Neolithic or early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, was found near Bottighofen and Romanshorn.</p><p>The Roman geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomponius-mela">Pomponius Mela</a>, around 43 CE, called the upper lake Lacus Venetus and the lower Lacus Acronius. Around 75 CE Pliny the Elder named both Lacus Raetiae Brigantinus after the main Roman town on the lake, Brigantium, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bregenz">Bregenz</a>, a name linked to the Celtic Brigantii. In English and the Romance languages the lake is named after the city of Constance, from the Roman emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a>, while the German name Bodensee derives from the village of Bodman.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The abbey island of Reichenau is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> for its three early medieval churches, and is also known for intensive cultivation of fruit and vegetables. The lake supplies municipal waterworks around its shores and the Bodensee-Wasserversorgung. The Alpine Rhine and the Seerhein do not mix greatly with the lake's water but flow through it on courses that change little.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lake Constance” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the crisis of the mid-3rd century, around 259–260, Rome abandoned the fortified Upper German and Raetian Limes, and the Alemanni gradually settled the land between the Rhine, the Danube and <b>Lake Constance</b>, the heartland of later Swabia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By the end of the century Stuttgart had become a centre of engineering: Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach built petrol engines in Cannstatt, Robert Bosch opened his workshop in Stuttgart in 1886, and Count Ferdinand von Zeppelin flew his first rigid airship over <b>Lake Constance</b> in 1900.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Count Ferdinand von Zeppelin's LZ 1 made its maiden flight from a floating hangar on <b>Lake Constance</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-2">2 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His descendants divided into several lines, including Werdenberg, Feldkirch, Bregenz and Tettnang, holding lands around the eastern shore of <b>Lake Constance</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-montfort">County of Montfort</a></li><li><blockquote>After their fall, it was recognised as an imperial city by Rudolf I around 1282, on the road from Ulm to <b>Lake Constance</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-biberach">Free Imperial City of Biberach</a></li><li><blockquote>Buchhorn grew on the north shore of <b>Lake Constance</b> as a fishing and market settlement under the counts of Buchhorn and later the Guelphs and Hohenstaufen.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-buchhorn">Free Imperial City of Buchhorn</a></li></ul></section>
